## E2 (L4) — The hedge as slope

You are long 200 units of asset A. Asset B is a candidate hedge. From
historical daily returns (real or synthetic), compute the ratio
vol(A)/vol(B). If you short $k$ units of B, your combined P&L slope to B's
price is $-k$. 

(a) With dollar exposures equal (k chosen so $k·P_B = 200·P_A$), plot one
year of the combined daily P&L. 
(b) In the market model language of the lesson: what role is $k$ playing,
and what would make it a *good* hedge beyond matching sizes?

```python
from qrc.data import get_prices
from qrc.synth import synthetic_prices

if DATA_SOURCE == "real":
    px = get_prices(["XLE", "SPY"], start="2018-01-01")
    A, B = "XLE", "SPY"
else:
    px = synthetic_prices(n_days=500, n_assets=2, seed=9)
    px.columns = [A, B] = ["A", "B"]
rets = px.pct_change().dropna()
# YOUR CODE
```

